    }

    public String processMessage(String messageId, String messageType, String content) {
        // Process a single message with the appropriate handler
    }

    public Map<String, String> processQueue() {
        // Process all messages in the queue
    }

    public void enqueueMessage(String messageId, String messageType, String content) {
        // Add a message to the processing queue
    }

    private String findHandlerKey(String messageType) {
        // Find the appropriate handler key based on routing rules
    }
}
```

## Routing Rule Patterns

Routing rules use special pattern matching:
- `#` matches any sequence of characters (equivalent to `.*` in regex)
- `*` matches any sequence of non-dot characters (equivalent to `[^.]*` in regex)

## Message Format

Queued messages should be formatted as: `messageId|messageType|content`

## Constraints

1. Message IDs must be unique - duplicate IDs should return "Message already processed"
2. If no handler matches the message type, return "No handler found for message type: [type]"
3. Routing rules should be checked in the order they were added
4. Queue processing should handle messages in FIFO order

## Example Usage

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MessageProcessor processor = new MessageProcessor();

        // Add handlers
        processor.addHandler("uppercase", String::toUpperCase);
        processor.addHandler("reverse", s -> new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString());

        // Add routing rules
        processor.addRoutingRule("text.*", "uppercase");
        processor.addRoutingRule("reverse.*", "reverse");

        // Process single message
        System.out.println(processor.processMessage("id1", "text.greeting", "hello"));
        // Output: HELLO

        // Process duplicate message
        System.out.println(processor.processMessage("id1", "text.greeting", "hello"));
        // Output: Message already processed

        // Queue processing
        processor.enqueueMessage("id2", "reverse.test", "abcd");
        processor.enqueueMessage("id3", "text.test", "queue message");

        Map<String, String> results = processor.processQueue();
        System.out.println(results.get("id2")); // Output: dcba
        System.out.println(results.get("id3")); // Output: QUEUE MESSAGE
    }
}
